 The ProofRail port takes a versioned context envelope containing the task contract, parent snapshot, confirmed decisions, latest failure evidence, unresolved tickets, budgets, authorized targets, allowed effects and reference hashes. It returns external execution facts and candidate references, never authoritative task PASS. An `agent-runner-request` identifies a call by `requestId/runId/taskId/stepId/attempt`, binds immutable input through `workspaceHash/contextHash/parentSnapshotHash/authorizationHash/budgetHash`, and explicitly lists `allowedTargets/allowedEffects`. `authorizationHash` must bind exactly one authorization-grant `recordHash`; admission resolves that unique grant by hash, then evaluates the authorization ledger by `authorizationId` for active/unrevoked/unexpired state, and requires `runId/taskId/stepId/allowedTargets` to match the request. `budgetHash` must bind this request's cost `reservationHash`, not a `ledgerHash` or allocation hash; the reservation must remain outstanding (no settlement), and its `runId/requestId/authorizationHash` must match the request. `allowedEffects` are operation effect IDs while grant `effectClasses` are risk classes. Each requested operation must exist in the request-bound frozen mapping version, and every mapped class must occur in the grant's `effectClasses`; grant supersets are allowed. Unknown, missing, or tampered mapping versions or hashes, unknown operations, and class under-coverage fail closed before replay identity consumption or dispatch. These binding semantics are frozen jointly by the Schema, canonical vector, semantic fixtures, and Go admission, but this does not mean the T027 production AgentRunner is implemented or that AT-23 passes. Production admission is a pure preflight and immutable binding: it must not consume request replay identities, publish R/C or write to the replay store, or launch processes; first-dispatch eligibility and replay identity consumption belong to the dispatch path and are decided after R has been published, based on the replay store; the admission verdict is a point-in-time preflight conclusion, not a lock, and may expire before dispatch, so after publishing R and before allowing launch the dispatch path must reconfirm that the authorization has not been revoked and that the budget reservation is still outstanding, failing closed otherwise, and a prior admission pass must never be treated as a launch permit. `mode=create` cannot carry a prior session; its first event establishes the request's sole session binding. `mode=resume` must bind `priorSessionId` and `priorCompletionHash`. Persist the request and authorization digest before launch. The same requestId and digest may replay, while a different digest conflicts; duplicate requests must not restart an agent in an unknown state or reapply the same candidate. A new business attempt receives new identity and cost accounting. Resume binds the original attempt, session and workspace; when continuity cannot be proven, reconstruct from the durable envelope as a new attempt rather than guessing that resume succeeded. Missing links, duplicates, digest mismatches, settled reservations, expired/revoked grants, or scope mismatches fail closed.
 
-Before any AI-assisted entry point starts, ProofRail must perform a fail-closed availability preflight for the provider profile and channel that will actually be used. A profile stores only provider type, base URL, model, transport options, and an opaque `secretRef`; API keys and tokens cannot enter configuration, digests, logs, errors, or evidence, and may be resolved only from the system SecretStore for temporary injection into the child-process environment. `agent-runner-cli`, `sessionbridge-silent`, and `sessionbridge-visible` each produce an independent `available`, `unavailable`, or `unknown` verdict that cannot be inherited by another channel. Valid configuration, an existing secret, an installed CLI/extension, reachable IPC, or successful visible delivery is insufficient to prove AI availability. `available` requires a fresh, evidence-backed minimal live response on that channel, bound to the profile configuration digest, channel, and probe time, and executed within an explicit request budget (the CLI-side credit bound is a candidate-platform fact that may exceed the product request budget; the actual argument is proven by the argv digest of the probe evidence, while the product request budget is still enforced independently and the request count is recorded). **This record carries no cost dimension, and that absence must not be read as "no cost was incurred"** — actual CLI-side billing and call counts are recorded by the candidate platform; the product side does not double-count them and makes no guarantee about the monetary bill; stale, timed-out, skipped, or incomplete evidence blocks. Failure reasons include at least `configuration_invalid`, `secret_missing`, `credential_rejected`, `account_unavailable`, `quota_exhausted`, `network_unreachable`, `tls_or_proxy_failure`, `provider_unavailable`, `model_unavailable`, `policy_blocked`, `cli_unavailable`, `sessionbridge_unavailable`, `lm_api_unavailable`, `visible_delivery_unavailable`, `response_invalid`, `timeout`, and `unknown`. An unavailable channel cannot automatically switch provider, model, or channel. Non-AI features may continue, while the affected AI entry point remains disabled and displays its structured reason.
