+For availability probing of the `agent-runner-cli` channel, the install source of the probe executable must be provably immutable: on Windows it is named by a stable absolute path and the ACL of its install directory denies write and delete to the current user's token; on Linux every component of its install directory path is not writable by the current user, and the executable is either ELF or its shebang interpreter satisfies the same immutability condition; all other platforms fail closed. The proof is persisted as the store-local `ai-install-source-proof.json` artifact under a path derived from the chain configuration directory (written by the ProofRail check command, read by the check command (re-read before every start) and the probe executor (re-verifies path, file identity, and digest live before every start); callers cannot choose its path). If the install source cannot be proven, `available` must not be recorded and the verdict is refused as `cli_unavailable`. Windows ACL and Linux mode/owner evaluation are owned by platform-specific implementations, and any failed evaluation fails closed. On Windows, the root name chain of that install-source path (every component below the volume root) must be classified component by component as "opened as itself", and a junction, symlink or any other reparse marker is refused; UNC, extended and relative paths are likewise refused. This criterion binds only the name chain of the evidence binding; it is not interchangeable with any existing statement about the spawn name.
 
 A `windows-credential:<target>` reference uses a generic credential in the current user's Windows Credential Manager with local-machine persistence; it is neither runtime isolation nor proof of provider availability. `prfrail secret set` may read a secret only from a verified local console with echo disabled; argv, redirection, pipes, JSON request bodies, and ordinary stdin injection are forbidden. `status` returns only the reference and existence, `delete` is idempotent, and none of the three commands displays the secret. An adapter receives only a temporary resolved byte copy and clears it after use. System failures return stable classifications without credential-blob content. Non-Windows platforms fail closed until their own SecretStore backend exists; they do not share or infer Windows credentials.
 
